const body = `<h1>5.2c — Enemy Factory: Scaling Monsters by Level</h1>

<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Expand Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Build: Enemy Class with Scaling</h3>
        <p>In RPGs, enemies scale with player level. A level-1 goblin has 10 HP; a level-10 goblin has 100. You'll build a constructor system that handles this automatically.</p>
        <p>Create a class called <code>Enemy</code>:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Instance variables: <code>String type</code>, <code>int level</code>, <code>int hp</code>, <code>int maxHp</code>, <code>int attack</code>, <code>int defense</code>.</li>
            <li>Constructor that takes <code>type</code> and <code>level</code>.</li>
            <li>Calculate stats based on type AND level:
                <ul>
                    <li>Goblin: base HP=10, ATK=3, DEF=2 — scale by 1.5× per level</li>
                    <li>Skeleton: base HP=15, ATK=5, DEF=3 — scale by 1.3× per level</li>
                    <li>Dragon: base HP=50, ATK=12, DEF=8 — scale by 2.0× per level</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li>Overloaded constructor: takes <code>type</code> only — defaults to level 1.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Verify Scaling</h3>
        <p>Create a level-1 goblin and a level-5 goblin. Print their stats side by side. Does the scaling make sense? Is a level-5 goblin roughly 5× as strong?</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
